# Day 1 Theory – HTML Structure

What is HTML?
HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.
It is the skeleton of every website. HTML only describes structure and content. It does not style the page or make it interactive.

# Semantic HTML
Semantic tags tell the browser and developers what the content actually means.

<header> → Top section of the page, used for logo, name, navigation
<nav> → Navigation links, used for menus
<main> → Main content of the page, the important part
<section> → A thematic group of content, used for About, Skills, Projects, etc.
<footer> → Bottom of the page, used for copyright and links
<h1> to <h6> → Headings, used for hierarchy
<p> → Paragraph, used for text blocks
<ul> and <li> → Unordered list, used for skills or project lists
<a> → Link, used for navigation and external links

# Non-semantic tags like <div> and <span> have no meaning.
We avoid them when a semantic tag exists.

# Why Structure Matters:
1.Better for SEO, because Google understands your page more easily
2.Better for accessibility, because screen readers can understand the layout
3.Easier to style later with CSS
4.Cleaner and more professional code
5.Quick Rules You Should Remember
6.Use only one <h1> per page, usually your name
7.Use <section> for major parts of the page
8.Always put content inside proper tags
9.Keep your code indented properly for readability
10.Every opening tag needs a closing tag

